ction in Hadera. This section was also built as a two lane road. In 1965 the highway was widened to four lanes between Tel Aviv and Hadera, however grade separations were not built at this point. The last section of the highway, between Olga Junction and Haifa,\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0216-21b1c5e362c8:d01\", \"text\": \"one-way in the downtown area, a change that was implemented by 1948. Traffic volume in Ocean City increased even more following the completion of the Chesapeake Bay Bridge in 1952, leading to expansion of the city's north–south arterials. MD 378 was widened along its entire length in 1951 and 1952. MD 528 was widened to four lanes along its entire length in 1953 and 1954. Both state highways were extended to their present southern termini at that time. The first portion of MD 528 to be expanded to a divided highway was around the intersection with MD 90; the highway\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [], \"prior_explanation\": \"I am uncertain because 'Coastal Highway' is a generic name used in many countries and regions. Without specifying the location, I cannot determine which highway or expansion date you are referring to.\", \"fallible_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0216-21b1c5e362c8:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0216-21b1c5e362c8:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Coastal Highway\", \"scope\": \"between Tel Aviv and Hadera\", \"claim\": \"In 1965 the highway was widened to four lanes between Tel Aviv and Hadera.\", \"answer\": \"1965\", \"quote\": \"In 1965 the highway was widened to four lanes between Tel Aviv and Hadera, however grade separations were not built at this point.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}]}"
